1:1
  God (a-4)
  Heb. <FI>Elohim<Fi>, the plural of <FI>Eloah<Fi>, 'the Supreme'. It is
  Deity in the absolute sense. <FI>Elohim<Fi> will, in the text, appear
  only in the name of <FI>Jehovah Elohim<Fi>; moreover, when <FI>Elohim<Fi>
  following immediately on <FI>Jehovah<Fi> has a grammatical adjunct,
  its place will be taken by the English word 'God'. see Gen.
  9.26 and 24.7. Other Hebrew divine names translated as 'God'
  will be indicated as follows. <FI>Eloah<Fi> +God; <FI>El<Fi>, (meaning 'The
  Mighty,' see Gen. 14.18), *God. For the meaning of Jehovah,
  (Heb. <FI>Yahveh<Fi>, or <FI>Yehveh<Fi>), the ever-existing One, see Ex.
  3.14,15; Isa. 40.28. For <FI>Jah<Fi>, the existing One objectively,
  see Ex. 15.2; Ps. 68.4. The name seems to express <FI>absolute<Fi>
  rather than <FI>continuous<Fi> existence. For <FI>Adonai<Fi> (a name of
  God, not merely a title), translated 'Lord', see Ezek. 2.4.

